import java.util.IdentityHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

/**
 * 128-bit digests of learned-state keys, shared by their owners' digest indexes and the planning read log.
 *
 * <p>
 * The logical, residual and applicability keys already carry a SHA-256 digest of their external form, which encodes
 * exactly the fields their {@code equals} compares; a key's 128 bits are the first 32 hex digits of it, parsed (no
 * allocation). A physical event key has no stored digest: its digest is a Merkle digest of its origin graph (kind,
 * source and frame of every node, in edge order), mixed with its current frame. Event-key equality is an isomorphism of
 * the origin graphs with equal kinds, sources and frames and an equal current frame, so equal keys unfold to the same
 * tree and get the same digest. A composite key's digest mixes its components' digests under a tag per key space. Equal
 * keys always get equal digests; the converse holds up to collisions, and every index built on these digests answers a
 * collision as an extra key under the digest.
 */
